Plasma Flow through Electromagnetic Shock Wave

As is explained in the main text, locally, we can neglect the transverse variation of the electromagnetic field in the shock wake. Directing the $x$-axis of the local coordinate system along the electric field in the shock, and the $y$-axis along the magnetic field, we express the field in the shock through the vector potential $\textnormal{\boldmath$A$}=(A(\xi),0,0)$ such that $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.1} E_x(\xi) = B_y(\xi) = -A'(\xi), \end{aligned}$$ where the prime denotes the derivative with respect to the argument. The electric and magnetic fields in the shock wake are proportional to $\delta(\xi-\xi_0)$, which means that $A$ is the step function, $A(\xi) \propto h(\xi-\xi_0)$; however, in the derivation below we treat $A(\xi)$ as an arbitrary function of its argument.

Consider an electron located in front of the field where $A=0$ with an initial momentum $(p_{x0}, p_{y0}, p_{z0})$. As the electron begins to interact with the field its momentum changes according to the equation of motion, $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.2} \frac{d\textnormal{\boldmath$p$}}{dt} = -\textnormal{\boldmath$E$}-\textnormal{\boldmath$v$}\times \textnormal{\boldmath$B$}, \end{aligned}$$ from which we find that $d{p}_y/dt=0$ (we remind the reader that the negative sign on the right-hand side of this equation takes into account the negative electron charge). Hence $p_y$ is an integral of motion, $p_y = p_{y0} = {\rm const}$. For the motion in $x$-direction we have $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.3} \frac{dp_x}{dt} = -E_x+v_zB_y = (1-v_z)A' = \frac{d}{dt}{A}, \end{aligned}$$ where we have used the relation ${d}{A}/{dt} = (\partial_t + v_z\partial_z)A = (1-v_z)A'$. Hence $p_x-A = {\rm const}$ and taking into account the initial values $A=0$ and $p=p_{x0}$, we obtain $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.4} p_x = A + p_{x0}. \end{aligned}$$

We now turn to the equation for $p_z$. We have $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.5} \frac{d}{dt}{p_z} = -v_xB_y = v_xA' = \frac{1}{2\gamma} \left[ (A^2)' + 2A'p_{x0} \right], \end{aligned}$$ where we have used $v_x=(1/\gamma) (A+p_{x0})$. It is also useful to write down the equation for the $\gamma$-factor, $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.6} \frac{d}{dt}{\gamma} = -v_xE_x = -v_xB_y = \frac{d}{dt}{p_z} \end{aligned}$$ from which it follows that $\gamma-{p_z}={\rm const}=\gamma_0(1-v_{z0}),$ and $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.7} \gamma^{-1} = (1-v_z)\gamma_0^{-1}(1-v_{z0})^{-1}, \end{aligned}$$ where $\gamma_0$ is the initial value of $\gamma$ before the acceleration. We will now show that the two terms on the right-hand side of Eq. ([eq:A.5]) are full time derivatives. For the first term, we have $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.8} \frac{1}{\gamma}(A^2)' &= \frac{1}{\gamma_0(1-v_{z0})}\left(1-v_z\right)(A^2)' = \frac{1}{\gamma_0(1-v_{z0})}\frac{d A^2}{d t}. \end{aligned}$$ A similar transformation shows that the second term is proportional to $dA/dt$. This allows us to integrate Eq. ([eq:A.5]) over time: $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.9} p_z &= \frac{1}{2\gamma_0(1-v_{z0})} ( A^2+2Ap_{x0} ) + \gamma_0 v_{z0}. \end{aligned}$$

Having solved a single particle motion through the shock wave, we will now consider what happens to the plasma flow when it crosses the shock. Let $n_{e0}$ be the initial plasma density. Because we neglect the transverse derivatives, the continuity equation [eq:5] reduces to $\partial_\xi [n_e(1-v_z)]=0$, from which we find $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.10} n_e(1-v_z) = n_{e0}(1-v_{z0}). \end{aligned}$$ As was mentioned in Section 2, there may be a multi-stream flow in the plasma—in this case Eq. [eq:A.10] is valid for each stream.

We are also interested in the jump of the transverse flows $n_ev_x$ and $n_ev_y$ through the shock wave. Using Eq. [eq:A.4] we obtain $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.11} n_ev_x = \frac{ A + \gamma_0 v_{x0} }{\gamma(1-v_z)} n_{e0} (1-v_{z0}) = n_{e0} v_{x0} + n_{e0} \frac{1}{\gamma_0} A, \end{aligned}$$ where we have used [eq:A.7]. We also have $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.12} n_ev_y = n_{e0} \frac{1-v_{z0}}{\gamma(1-v_z)} \gamma_0v_{y0} = n_{e0} v_{y0} \end{aligned}$$ The last two equations can be combined into a vectorial one, $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.13} n_e\textnormal{\boldmath$v$}_\perp - n_{e0}\textnormal{\boldmath$v$}_{\perp 0} = \frac{n_{e0}}{\gamma_0} \textnormal{\boldmath$A$}, \end{aligned}$$ where $\textnormal{\boldmath$v$}_\perp$ and $\textnormal{\boldmath$A$}$ are two-dimensional vectors with $\textnormal{\boldmath$A$}$ defined by $$\begin{aligned} \label{eq:A.14} \textnormal{\boldmath$A$}(\xi) = - \int_{-\infty}^\xi d\xi' \textnormal{\boldmath$E$}_\perp(\xi'), \end{aligned}$$ where $\textnormal{\boldmath$E$}_\perp(\xi)$ is the transverse electric field in the shock wave. Eq. [eq:A.13] is valid everywhere inside the shock— we will use it for calculation of the jump in the transverse plasma flow through the shock wake taking the limit $\xi\to\infty$ in Eq. [eq:A.14].

 1. The idea of fitting the plasma wakefields by formulas where $r_b$ is increased by $k_p^{-1}$ was proposed to the author by V. Lebedev.↩



What equations are derived for the calculation of the longitudinal wake?<|assistant|><think>

</think>


Based on the document, the key equations derived for the calculation of the longitudinal wake inside the plasma bubble are:

1) Equation (23), which expresses the jump in the longitudinal electric field $\Delta E_z$ behind the source charge in terms of the function $D(r,\xi_0)$ that characterizes the strength of the electromagnetic shock wave:

$\Delta E_z(r,\xi_0) = \int_r^\infty dr' D(r',\xi_0) \frac{n_{e0}(r',\xi_0)}{\gamma_0(r',\xi_0)}$

2) Equation (30), which gives an expression for the longitudinal wake potential $w_l$ on the axis in terms of $\Delta \hat{E}_z$, which is an integral of $D(r)$:  

$w_l = \frac{2\hat{E}_z(r_b)}{r_b} \left(1+\frac{r_b}{2}\Delta \hat{E}_z(r_b)\right)^{-1}$

So in summary, the key equation is (23), which relates the longitudinal wakefield to the function $D(r)$ characterizing the strength of the shock wave. Equation (30) gives a specific formula for the wake potential on the axis in terms of $D(r)$.<|user|>
