if Walt Whitman, hired as a Don Cherry replacement, were asked to whip up some patriotic fervour before the big game, & took the opportunity to wax poetic re: his summer cabins. __

sing O

for a garrulous garrison nation

transcend

the hairy limits of invention's leg

gird up your inward granite threads of spine

scale sheer affinity,

& set your best spelunking lights to "singleness of purpose"

assume this fractious crouch snap at some heels  
like Leash'd in like hounds, yelp

"fresh and glorious," "profuse bloomings," and "refulgent moons"

(o thanks for asking—as it happens—

I just got this chorus off some summer Pas

who, dozing in their Adirondack chairs,

do hem me in & hold me up)

"O vasty fields!" "O woody and rocky islands!"

"O handsome young fellows and girls!"

there's no beyond the lands I've garbled up  
my range's piney scope of homes

there's no beyond these pages, no beyond my vasty coasts

where shrill and temporary ships

their engines roiling tirelessly

do sunder warmish seas

look here: within these summer leaves I've caught

the endless nation queries flung my way—

Why should one thousand tracts be give one man?

For simple digging of a fire-pit hole?

Why must we stock our vicious children full of tins?

(speak quick, the torch gets pitchy)

and as my crooked little finger does Attest,

there's decimation much—  
historical catastrophes—there's often much to say—

such as, of late, some

well-rehearsed and boreal dismay —

don't let that stuff on your imagination work—

consider _my_ staked claim

Admit _my_ habitat into this history

let me & galloping carnage off the hook

yon lake-side legions do approve my swelling seams,

with every ogling eyeball peeled

so why don't you?
if Walt Whitman were to anachronistically imagine the memoirs of an early 20th century ancestor who emigrated to the Canadian Northwest to live in a sod house, sort of à la Laura Ingalls Wilder.

land sakes I've often

shot at drowsy billowed gnats

across the open atmosphere

across the many shapes that season nonexistent villes

I came, I dreamed a prairie elevatored

homesick for my homesick dog

I said, & spread my arms so wide,

this here fair land's my first fat chance

goose feathers gamey buckshot milk

a prairie chicken supper

mornings frost would melt inside

fall from the grassroots to the bedsheets

soak sodden walls with condensation

of course,

of my most-loved story,

of my fourteen children,

of my humble style,

of my eventful simplicity,

of my settler life—

I cannot put everything in
the mother of old

my mother's generations

my mother in my belly

the white heads of the old mothers

old men and mothers

a young mother and an old mother

a girl and a wife

a mother's laps

all those sea-voyages, and all those men and women

all those who left the well-beloved northern parts behind

and all the father-stuff

my grandmother's father the sailor

my father and his friends

a thirty years' apprenticeship

a calfskin book

some golden lettering

some stylish music

those mothers, and the mothers of their mothers

almost three hundred years ago

and all those chore boys at the Wolf & Co.

twelve years old and dozing in the daylight

their legs went back and forth between a dozen well-stocked stores

the fishing village and its blustery days

our love of music and our good clear singing voices

it was our home

the cot in mother's bedroom

all those mothers of men

and further out where they kept the sheep

hundreds of sheep in high meadows at nighttime

my dog and I alone all night in spring

bright nights

in summertime

they milked the sheep

births and births

and other births

and hurry home, give birth

my grandmother's golden watch

for 160 acres we left it all,

my sisters and myself

the sea was hard to cross and then

so many of us were ill

at last we got just ten miles south of where we'd have to go

the food was for the most part difficult

salt herring eggs boiled blue hard bread

and beds that crawled with bugs

but now look at us,

here we are

and now I know a running skunk's no cat

and now I love tomatoes, too
if Walt Whitman got a job writing spam for "fast fashion" retailers and environmental NGOs, & while at work took full advantage of the Oxford English Dictionary Word of the Day emails and also Shakespeare's sonnets.

My veggie nods to me & I to him—

all unashamed I slake my homestead thirst

& Make my quarter-section face in public

A brief and pointed sunshiney request—

I shimmy like a prospector, shake it

like a survey horse's bruised feet, pop at 'em

like the rush of cattle to a bug smudge

My arsenal, so gently ticklish, changes

chemic state; like every native milkweed

with its tender dancing hips

My saucy jacks, so happy are in this,

we all rush in like lawyers to embrace Big Ag

awesome is ending, so let's go wild—

we got just hours left

Grasshoppers clicking, ladies

Now here's the best good news­—it's humpback whale month!

Have you got yours?

Have you picked out your mesh empire-line dress?

This swan song's superhot.

I'm hard.

I don't _need_ to be hard.

The truth behind my strappy sandals is: I just don't _need_ to.

But I am.

Look, if I find I'm drowning in my dahlia lace collection I'll just

pack enough bad ass into these bodysuits

& cash in my sweet geese: great missile savings on some

boiling hot Defense

The next six hours are critical: spring's gonna pop, pop metal-like,

pop real pink

Quick: get more than half-off all your military sauce

Get over here, come quick,

salute me: Sporty Classic comrade-style
Hell, Kathleen, this is it: it's my last chance

to reach all of Ontario. It's summer,

I'm replete & steeping in errata,

got my tongue fluent in the motherese.

Come on down, sweetcheeks: it's my last night in Nice!

If you get wood by midnight then you're Welcome:

help us defend our climate, put paint on

something live, smooth out your creases. Be sudden

and amazing, Shine bright like the new day—

lighted streams, revitalize fine urban

destinations. Cell phones spread like democracy

(by parthenogenesis); our masses

are massive, our Beauty takes neither prisoners

nor shortcuts.

My seat is sizzling, I'm all ready to adopt a cub.

Don't miss your chance to make a difference

in the world / 'cause you were soaking in it.

So tell me, what's your peace priority?

An Arctic adventure? Topsiders?

One more last-minute mid-summer blowout?

Bite like a flea. Score a killer weekend.

The mule's this summer's "it" beast of burden

& all our alma mater trekkers get a sneak peek

or up to 25% off Everest just for being you.

At railway crossings, watch as fallow green jocks bring

the hot miles crash together. No Canadian warplanes.

No spring fling. No flip-flop emendations.

That's just the way it goes: my Sunday best

gets happy action from my Thursday afternoons.

And baby, while you're up, could you spring clean

a wildlife refuge for me real quick? 
Connect with megafauna in YOUR area code,

smooth over national wounds &

enter the lace race before it gets too late

Double your impact, double your agronomy, have ALL the seal

food trios:

climb Canada's boldest prints, enjoy green justice. you DESERVE

this deal.

Discover a new economy, touch base with big polluters NOW,

fall into floral frenzies, sprout

Make it to Canada Place for noon,

Migrate with butterfly finesse, flourish your collective see-through

bustle

sign five petitions for nine aromatic rhinos

Make sure you don't miss out on charming blowholes, violent

arachnids,

& the season's most-watched ventral grooves
as I was eavesdropping in luscious ferns, swanning 'round great

swags of fungus,

florid in my annual intimates,

I heard someone say there's a decline in electric eel faucets,

the numbers are down on Canadian shields,

& lounging on docks with unbelievable threadcounts got pallid

on the other hand, there's been an uptick in widening guy-wires

& yawping barbells

—nothing new in pink glaciers or remote-controlled helicopters—

but it's looking good for reptiles (think long-term toothless

reparations)

So call 'em up, my friends at supper, go & tell 'em:

next season's your last chance

to Grow a new soul, get some dignity,

see winter's sunny haven

The best are tightest in their prints & patterns

the worst can't even do appropriation

when it's time to honour Mater, make sure

to splash out on some real good river Tea:

she'd just be thrilled. you know that what she really wants is

lamentations, the Laurentians,

some halfway decent ministerial

gyrations. you know I've got it on pretty good

authority that what she really wants

is microdermabrasion, a geothermal spirit bear

(accessible, _s.v.p._, in his own natural habitat)

a squared-off watermelon head

a flustered summer oyster

grateful old-growth forest

and a positron to match

I have to admit it, I'm slightly terrific:

espied by cavalcades of well-read & summer-ready women,

I'm salving myself with hot midsummer toys,

I'm listing like a caddis fly

or else another of summer's sweltering envious ones

You know, Kathleen, I'd just as soon win all the books by women,

fail the Northern Gateway test,

or buy a babydoll for merely 97 cents

My blatant friendly tum, here's splendid garden eels for you:

a love-back half-off price concession—

50% off more than half of Canada

& after all, I stand with science—why don't you?
WE NEED YOUR HELP: give us your fingertips

before the monarchs head back north again

in search of wide-calf boots, chic children's clothes,

a special chicken offer

my dudes, you _know_ that GREEN OIL

won't just nimbly leap into your outstretched song
let's hang out, therefore, here: inside the tender hollow of my hand

it's maybe not too late. there may be news to save us,

like several tiny newly hatched-out bees

born in November out of the joint of time

sheltered from our toxic deluge

from our gentle staggering gait
so come check out the Last Best Place on Earth, where flesh-flies rush

like water from the inlet. diversity means so much less of

Everything

our clear-cut logging plan is crazy-good. new lows like: WHOAH

we've got just what you need so: PILE ON

we've got just what you're looking for: new amphibian

showstoppers,

an environmental assault that'll hit the ground in layers

we've done it again: we cool we slim we straight

we know the 15 secrets of the new fat shrimp
if Walt Whitman were a wealthy Vancouver resident bobbing about in a life raft in the suddenly much deeper Burrard Inlet; & if the occasion of this bobbing led to reminiscences about the creation of the city, via Major Matthews' Early Vancouver archives (re: the "purchasing" of land in the late 19th century) and Jean Barman's historical research (on the destruction of the Kitsilano Reserve in the early 20th).

and then the water came way up.

it came way up across the beach

we watched it from our home's great open view

we watched it looking south towards the sun

(the temperature, we'd often said, grows limpider each year

we'd turn & wisely shake our grizzled heads)

the water kept on coming, rolling glorious

and as it came across the sand, the logs began to float

the water slid up past the beach it came across the causeway

swelled past the length of our empire perennials

began to sink our patio materials

drowned all our bright and shining thing-like objects

the water kept on coming—as it came, like sudden joy,

accompaniments came: dead dogs bird cages burning bits of wood

the water kept on coming and each house began to flood

we stirred—leapt downstairs with our full-informed considerations

cached all our wealth behind the metal-clad facades

in families, we portioned out our faults

and then, our breath all cautious in our nostrils, we took

to all our small boats, and we swayed,

as all our little hulls were carried higher

as in the odd light we did gently rattle oarlocks

our ample midrise balconies now float

our grand three-bedrooms snags for nesting birds
we'll swamp one day, of course, as father alway said

his hands behind his head, awash in growths of finest clover

and well you know I can't be blamed for I was just a boy

we've always known that violent storms might come

at almost any time

each morning, when the tide recedes, we'll set out to our daily

chores,

to our activities, each carefully chosen for one of the emphasis areas

for the benefit of our very own though not quite brand new

neighbourhood

though things can sometimes look quite frightening

we'll put our comfort in our master-plan

our faith in visions brought to life

when we first landed, all those years ago, we came through

brackish waters

and it was sunlit where we ran aground

they did not see us, or they might have stopped us coming

we'd made our way through all this river aching

trudged through the very necklaces of pure sweet

eligible lakes

we saw a big, flat stretch we turned we turned into another arm

we knew we must avoid them at all costs

we knew they'd likely try to stop us coming

we gazed into the plenteous woods,

sunk low on rafts, crept overhand onto our flights of logs

we knew (we reasonably knew) that we could only progress slowly

consider a blackbird, his faded gentility:

we'd introduce these fellows, set them up, and let them spread

these are the kinds of incremental changes and responsive actions

that I mean

& soon, you know, these white-browed railway fellows—

I mean, the public—soon they were able to enjoy the forest safely,

& gracefully meander through its systematic groves,

as they do now

(or used to do—

I'm sure they will again)
I called it then an unsurpassed habitat

I called it then a grand, sane, temperate land

when, glittering like the studs upon your belt, you loaded up

your old & muzzled gun

I gazed upon a wide array of biological features, I made an excited

little speech

I saw your photograph in highland costume, backlit against a

subtle shot

—arboreal features (I mean the forest)

I heard this monstrous sound

(wild animals?) out on the flatland by the river

home, then, to cougar bear and elk

I shivered then and yes, I thought, you must have been in the war

in one of the gunships

in "the place around which there are hurried and joyous waters,

continually"

you know, just a small gun, about 24 or 30 inches long

with a bore big enough to drop an apple into

we always knew our progress would depend on management

my critical assessment was—I saw, or I imagined I could see

