each shining, shining face run down to water—

our town sprang up—our sure foundation-nutriment

great sawmills down the riverbank—The Dominion, The Canadian

White Pine—

they might have stopped us coming then

but now they'd never try it

they'd never try to move us now

we're stuck here now like limpets
and now the greater part of us are old and feeble men

on pleasant afternoons we sit, and slowly turn towards the light

like the broad yellow faces of sunflowers

as vigorous youths we did correct the dangerous trees

removed all arboreal apprehension (I mean the fear of trees)

raised up our own trunks in our own Dominion

oh sure, there's one or two went all to pieces on the river bank—

just rotted

back when the woods were full of fir, and spruce, and pine, and cedar

through the summers, through the winters—

the hardy influence of winters—

our due proportion—

we fostered silence, bred civility

brought in abundance of the river

made all of its surrounding pasture
strong as a live-bodied oak, as a soft-bodied animal

us rangers worked all ceaseless to improve the forests,

the rivers,

the many Mountains of Canada:

increase validity, improve structures, set up remedies

as the moss hung down, we'd trace a form in shallow water

