congress, when laden down with nature's time,

becomes well-nigh impossible to grow, to pick, to bloom

a massive public outcry might be better

grafted on a pine tree

spring into action sprung

all weighted down with soggy paper vowels

shore up my shoulder's opposition—as in,

is it moving, earth? our only green delight?

shore up my oil, my industry, my allies,

shore 'em up with trump & tinny drum

this deepened intimate and ponderous dependence

might break out into thin and dirty fossil tunes

enriched quick handfuls scud full forward as

the earth-like zipper opens
the coastal range, I hear,'s been hit with eastern olive honey blossom

it's pretty bad I heard maybe we better lope perniciously

upon these wet & slippery coasts?

let's maybe face this moment critically, head on?

face it together?

you know if we could lick up all that oil just think of what we'd

save on costly piston modules?
a hundred-inch widescreen projector shows me you,

my music, playing like the tides

The truth is the Americans can get our books, and do

but don't you let me taint the air with chirped old-time declensions

I'd do much better to reign in these shiny lips of mine

or hang upon collapse's neck like happens,

chance, that dainty copper coin, she'll never miss

but always hears the decimal's soft ping of introduction
gold chains encircle summer's basic address

a carpenter hammers a hell-kettle

a local campaign founders a system of holes appears

his foot slipped, the twig broke, and he was precipitated into this

dreadful foolish people

a moose fell in a sink-hole & got shot (there's justice)

apparently, like earnestness,

a natural feature's bottomless
if Walt Whitman indulged in some exquisite shame as he stretched out on the lawn of his friends' stately home in Southern Ontario; & if his sense of complicity added a frisson to an already lovely summer evening (robin song, scented flowers, long tree shadows, et al.); if Walt Whitman were more or less his traditional self.

flung out upon this ample, charming lawn

I indulge myself in a thousand, lingering, twilight shames—

rose-coloured shames

I lap them up in this most flavoured air. these, my usual small and

shining shames,

are mellow, rich, and delicate.

the fullness of my vivid shame's my beauty.

like the clustered motion

of three soft hens

basking in the evening's fading light

in the tall and narrow shadow of a turret

I stand, as if before such birds

I gaze, as if upon such bright and crimson beaks

and I embrace my shame, I cling it to myself

Give me your rosy shame,

say it aloud: shame, shame, shame, shame, shame—

a moist, voluptuating cry, of sympathy or pleasure

like a great many voices crying out all at once:

for shame, for shame—so musical, so pleasant!

shame is a funny old man in a hat

shame is the close of day in June

shame eats and shrinks your satin cloakes

these broad green grassy grounds

the forest floor's the happy home of many a shame-swoll'n toad

shamefish: a famous church for pretty girls

shame soothes, like a drummer's tepid march

shame-worthy, I could move to placid tears

shame's early, gently closing

the faintly given half

an infant satisfaction
if Walt Whitman were a graduate student working in the government publications section of Robarts Library, taking notes on a geological survey of Lake Superior.

and was I entering or

leaving this room?

the singular transcription of my out-door notes—

scratched on the backs of letters,

on home-made note-books

I might have left them out

I might have left them all outside

my notes on the Red Rock River fault

that most notable fault,

that active, wakeful fault

I hear that worries and doubts are common—

but still, I might have left them out

I sense the tremor of

a negative outcome, thick like dew on paperbacks  
I cannot tell how all this came into my hands  
how did it come into my hands?

a childhood onset, full on south,

from out around Old Woman Bay

what would it mean to act on thoughts

a stirring thought, a gentle thought

a piece of broken glass on the road  
out by the largest of the dikes

against the water's constant pressing

what if, in the autumn of 1900, all senselessness was lost

what if I could predict a similar catastrophe:

roadcuts, failures of personal well-being  
broken glass

I count Pillow lavas and stacks  
a growing sound, something impending,

somewhere near Gargantua Harbour

what if I've made a terrible mistake

broken glass forms shapes  
like interesting cataracts

I consider the case of  
violent or aggressive thoughts—

I consider the older, Precambrian rock

unstable like cracked shale

I consider: it's just a couple pages more to the St. Lawrence

and if I wander continually  
I might just catch a faint, a fresh, a barely palpable air

consider the morality of introducing so many perennials

should they escape? what then?

the waterways all choked up?

Keweenawan lavas, too,  
occur so frequently

like Cat-birds, thrushes, robins, etc.

and is this down to me?

and is it true distortions lead me to experience

a substantially higher rate  
of exposure?

that blasphemous thoughts bloom like agates?

bloom like the profuse bloomings of the tiger lily (is it the tiger-lily?)

and is it true that blooms cause stream erosion—

could that be either cause or an effect?

and it is possible to clench one's jaw

on a first-class river steamer?

to be subsumed with worry

through numbered river valleys?

I note that Eskers persevere and isn't that enough?

I've heard it said

that Canada was worth it all  
tho' it may have had harmful consequences

Glacial spillways—

I know I have to do something—

more frequent exposures—

As I write, a long shell appears  
on the Baldhead

the weight of the glaciers has ceased to depress the level of the lakes

what am I afraid might happen?  
water is like frost: is that significant?  
is it bizarre, upsetting, or intrusive  
when frost shatters?

and could that sound just be the cracking trees?

I'm coming to resemble a relative of myself

I stride athwart each clouded stream, and—

what else could I tell you, tell myself,  
of all these half-burnt shells  
the debris or talus accumulated at their bases?

I could multiply some individual probabilities  
I could throw black shadows into strong relief  
wave to the action and the lake ice

give each of them a ranking  
is it clear, is it glistering water?

how very moral do we see ourselves to be?  
how very dashy and inspiriting?

as I stack shores and count sea caves, I consider my  
unwanted thoughts their  
generally jagged appearance the

probable outline of our  
final sea catastrophe

on a rocky shore, to and fro, one afternoon,

I may have forgotten my cigarette

I may have left it

left it there

I may have left it just outside  
and which of these dreaded events are reasonably possible?  
I compile a dossier, and a creek I draw a circle

I know that everyone has susceptibility to some disease  
even to an illness

I know that the Dominion keeps entire faith

It is critical to reorganize  
these beaches—ensure the waves suffice

keep careful notes  
include yourself last

feel yourself to be perfectly still and cloudless, fresh and cool  
smooth like Buckshot—

This is the effort to achieve perfection

but even in these, my own most pleasant hours,

unwanted thoughts, spangled like orbs,

still nestle in the bedrock, emerge like raised contemporary beaches

there is a little physical discomfort  
a residue, along the riverbed, its rocks

remember, coastal erosion is a feature of regional,

provincial, and even national significance

to fully experience the benefits,  
go out every day

Contaminate the other people

consider the eskers, how they are scattered   
smell the smell of freshcut herbage  
there: how much less do you dwell on it now?  
how much less do you suffer now?

allow the sprouting of new associations  
consider the possibility of not having toxicoplasmosis

such thoughts, completed  
offer a great place for our birds

this coldwater river contains  
varieties of minnows yes, the minnows are still here

they are still present

but will they always be?

note this down in your notes

in your well-fibred little forest notes

in Lake Superior there are sea lamprey eels: and ought one not

to be responsible for this  
Though but a looker on?

what of the insect populations?  
I hear the sound of scythes: like breaking, snapping

I try to remember that animal life in general is very representative  
it is highly persistent  
like the sandhill crane

I can convince myself beyond a doubt  
I am a peaceful person  
tho' perhaps less rugged

I try to avoid certain habits, like counting hawks

(I'm sure there were fewer yesterday)

the feeling of doubt

like a car running over broken glass

and are these features of perhaps provincial dread?

consider the native brook trout

aren't they desirable?

they have a larger and a fresher look?
if Walt Whitman were washing dishes in a kitchen in East Vancouver, listening to the coyotes through the screen door while her daughter slept in the other room.

it's late, i'm doing dishes in the company of moths

the streetlight's out between me and the park's black trees

between me and the dark there's a coyote killing something

my child's asleep in bed, curls dark in shadows and with sweat

eyelashes like mosquito legs

in sunlight i can almost see the lake behind the house but not right

now: it's dark

the streetlight's out again

a former bog, a skating rink, a mill pond, water for a brewery

they stopped up all the creeks to put the houses in

a sort of unattractive fish here now, an unattractive name

i read that once, before the creeks were filled, a seal could swim this whole way from the sea
if Walt Whitman concluded his evening with a few fingers of whiskey and the works of José de Acosta (on "natural and moral history") and John Locke (on "government").

In our discourse on plants we will end with those which are proper

and peculiar

It cannot be supposed plants should always remain in common and

uncultivated

Plants were given to the use of the Industrious and Rational (and

Labour was to be his Title to them)

THE PRODUCE MUST NOT SPOIL

THE GRASS OF HIS ENCLOSURE MUST NOT ROT UPON

THE SOIL

THE FRUIT OF HIS PLANTING MUST NOT PERISH

BUT BE GATHERED AND LAID UP 

