# notes

The following represents my best effort to list the texts that went into this book. Many, if not all, of the poems draw words and phrases from Walt Whitman's diary (and, to a lesser extent, from various editions of _Leaves of Grass_ —especially "Song of Myself").

The poems in this book also use words and phrases from assorted anti-anxiety and CBT worksheets; spam emails received by me over the last seven or eight years; the Oxford English Dictionary (including its Word of the Day email service); poems and plays by William Shakespeare (particularly _Henry V_ ); and Wikipedia entries. I regret having lost track of the online material that provided me with the names of the fishes that inhabit Lake Ontario.

"if Walt Whitman were a wealthy Vancouver resident" draws heavily on Major Matthews' _Early Vancouver._ This lengthy archival text contains material from a variety of interlocutors, including early Vancouver "pioneers," members of local Indigenous communities, and government officials. Some of the figures whose words appear here (in various forms) include Major Matthews himself, Chief Joe Capilano, King Edward VII, W. H. Gallagher, Major-General Richard Moody, and Mrs. John Morton. Of these, only Chief Joe Capilano and King Edward are named in the text. Page 62 of this poem also reproduces words and phrases from Jean Barman's research into the history of Vancouver. A number of phrases from the 2009 _Stanley Park Forest Management Plan_ made their way in here too.

"if Walt Whitman were a Canadian journalist" is a kind of loose parody of a 2014 Christie Blatchford column about a "land dispute" in Caledonia, Ontario.

"if Walt Whitman were a graduate student" contains material (mostly a series of nouns) from a geological survey of Lake Superior that I found in the Government Publications section of Robarts Library at the University of Toronto (but have since been unable to locate).

I am also grateful for my access to T.L.'s unpublished memoir of settling in Canada's Northwest in the early 20th century, a text uncertainly reflected in "if Walt Whitman were to anachronistically imagine." This narrative is not quite my inheritance, and it is somehow just that.

# About the Author

fenn stewart lives in Vancouver, where she teaches literature, writing, and research. Her poetry has appeared in _The Capilano Review, Open Letter, The Arcadia Project_, and in the form of three chapbooks, _An OK Organ Man_ (shortlisted for the 2013 bpNichol Chapbook Award), _Vegetable Inventory_, and _from Waltzing_. Her research on Canadian culture and literature has appeared in various journals, including _ARIEL: A Review of International English Literature_ and _Law, Culture, and the Humanities_. _Better Nature_ is Stewart's first book of poetry.
